[stderr] ## Acceptance criteria
[stderr] 
[stderr] - The initial collection contains only a bounded bootstrap wave; each
[stderr]   dispatcher expands at most one 250-contract domain shard, creates at most ten
[stderr]   new B-series authoring tasks plus one successor, then exits. It never attempts
[stderr]   to materialise the whole game plan.
[stderr] - There is exactly one valid B-series authoring runbook per allocated manifest
[stderr]   batch, and it creates no more than seven assigned R-series runbooks.
[stderr] - B-series runbooks never overwrite an existing R-series contract with a
[stderr]   conflicting ID; they stop and report mismatches.
[stderr] - Every generated B-series runbook includes its context packet, output list,
[stderr]   validation procedure, checkpoint/update procedure, and a clear stop condition.
[stderr] - Every generated B-series runbook states that it must author its allocated R
[stderr]   files despite unresolved implementation gates or unimplemented R
[stderr]   dependencies, retaining those gates in the R contract rather than stopping.
[stderr] - Both child-collection registration files exist, contain valid JSON with a
[stderr]   relative `runbooks_dir`, and point to the generated authoring and
[stderr]   implementation collections respectively.
[stderr] - R-series contracts use stable IDs and parse with the Supervisor runbook
[stderr]   loader; their declared dependencies match the manifest, every contract has
