l-tokens-and-responsive-behaviour\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#accessibility-policy\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0010\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0002\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No final artwork, fonts, or icons; semantic tokens only and prohibit incidental assets.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"non-colour cues\",\"reduced motion values\",\"GATE-0002,GATE-0004 retained\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and await numeric design decisions\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0011\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0011\",\"title\":\"Create original-content provenance control\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0005\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0011.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/01-product-brief.md#originality-boundary\",\"specification/04-experience-contract.md#experience-premise-and-originality-boundary\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0011\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0005\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets are created; this contract defines provenance controls for later asset-required work.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"missing provenance rejected\",\"human creative review recorded\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this contract\"},\n    {\"id\":\"R0012\",\"catalogue_id\":\"IMP-0012\",\"title\":\"Document foundation architecture and recovery boundaries\",\"dependencies\":[\"R0002\",\"R0004\",\"R0006\",\"R0008\"],\"output_path\":\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0012.md\",\"source_specifications\":[\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\"],\"source_catalogue_ids\":[\"IMP-0012\"],\"authoring_batch\":\"B0006\",\"factory_stages\":[\"F011\",\"F012\",\"F013\"],\"asset_impact\":\"not_applicable\",\"asset_ids\":[],\"asset_brief\":\"No assets; developer-documentation closure only.\",\"acceptance_checks\":[\"all foundation ownership/dependencies/gates cross-checked\",\"does not claim release readiness\"],\"checkpoint_cadence\":\"after metadata, after draft, before manifest acceptance\",\"recovery\":\"retain ID and repair only this documentation contract\"}\n  ]\n}\n# Product brief \u2014 Mock Fantasy Quest 4\n\n**Source of truth:** [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md). This normalized brief supports\ndownstream planning; it preserves source scope and records unknowns rather than\nchoosing them.\n\n## Product problem and outcome\n\nCasual adult players need a compact, approachable fantasy RPG that delivers a\ncomplete story without requiring a long commitment or dependable connection.\nThe first useful session is successful when a new player finishes a first\nturn-based battle and understands how to save and resume. The release outcome\nis an original short campaign that a player can complete through exploration,\ndialogue, quests, combat, rewards, saving, and a short ending.\n\n## Category, users, and core experience\n\n| Area | Normalized first-release statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Category | Deliberately small, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG. |\n| Users | Casual and adult players, including people using keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad, captions, adjustable text, and visual settings. |\n| Campaign boundary | One town, one dungeon, and one guided, short ending; no further regions or chapters are required. |\n| Core loop | Explore, converse, accept or advance a quest, enter party-based turn-based combat, manage rewards/inventory, save, and continue. |\n| Experience direction | Original warm hand-painted fantasy, readable silhouettes, and a restrained palette. |\n| Functional reference | *Final Fantasy V* may inform only the high-level scope of a party-based turn-based adventure. |\n\n## Selected target systems\n\n| Target | First-release role | Input model | Delivery constraints | Out-of-scope boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Responsive public web application | Primary browser surface for the complete game. | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad. | Responsive phone/tablet/desktop layout; browser compatibility and performance budget must be approved before implementation. | Native binaries, a separate web-only campaign, and browser-exclusive mechanics. |\n| Progressive web app (PWA) | Installable delivery of the same shared game core. | Same inputs where the browser/device supports them. | Offline assets and save-data behavior; safe update/cache refresh must preserve confirmed player data. | A PWA-exclusive campaign, mandatory continuous connection, or a different ruleset. |\n\nThe product uses a shared core with platform adaptation only where necessary.\nIt should work on widely used devices and under feasible slow-network\nconditions; exact supported browsers, device classes, storage quotas, and\nnetwork floor are open decisions.\n\n## First-release scope and measurable success measures\n\nIncluded scope: playable exploration; one town and one dungeon; narrative,\ndialogue, quests, progression and a short ending; onboarding/tutorial; at least\none tutorialized turn-based battle; inventory and rewards; save/load, resume,\ncheckpoints, data-loss recovery; settings and accessibility; audio/visual\nfeedback; content/level delivery; and crash reporting with player-facing error\nrecovery. The source also names account/identity, multiplayer/player safety,\nlive-service operations, and purchase/entitlement handling, but their release\nstatus remains contingent on the explicit conflict decision below.\n\nThe release is measurable when:\n\n- A new player can finish the tutorial battle, create a save, reload it, and\n  reach the short ending on each supported target.\n- The delivered campaign contains exactly one town, one dungeon, and one short\n  ending; added regions or chapters are not needed for release completion.\n- Both targets meet their stated input role; the PWA is installable and each\n  target has a tested offline, save, update, and error-recovery path.\n- Dialogue, inventory, quest, and progression UI are readable with approved\n  accessibility settings, and captions/visual settings are available where the\n  corresponding feedback exists.\n- An interruption, recoverable error, or safe update offers a player-facing\n  recovery path and does not silently discard the last confirmed save.\n\n## Later roadmap, non-goals, and reference boundary\n\nDeferred roadmap: live events/seasonal content, player-created/shared content\n(levels, mods, designs, stories), advanced analytics, and advanced\nmonetisation. They are excluded from first-release acceptance.\n\nNon-goals: copied branding, assets, text, layouts, characters, story/lore,\naudio, maps, or distinctive interactions; additional towns/dungeons/campaigns;\nnative apps; and platform-specific game variants. No cloud-sync, payment,\nmoderation, service-uptime, or backend commitment is implied before approval.\n\n| Reference boundary | Prohibited | Permitted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Branding and copy | Names, logos, trademarks, marketing copy, story text, dialogue, characters, and world/lore. | A high-level description of a compact party-based turn-based adventure. |\n| Assets and presentation | Art, music, sound, maps, screen composition, visual language, and layouts. | Original art and UI that fulfil this brief\u2019s own direction. |\n| Interaction design | Reproducing distinctive combat screens, menus, flows, quests, or interaction choreography. | Independently designed mechanics and feedback appropriate to the requested scope. |\n\n## Constraints and safe assumptions\n\nExplicit constraints: web/PWA delivery, offline play, accessibility,\nlocalisation, privacy, safe save/recovery, slow-network feasibility, and\noriginal expression. Safe working assumptions: the offline core does not block\non a remote service; content/UI are original; local persistence is durable\nenough for confirmed saves where browser storage permits; connected features,\nif approved, degrade visibly; and crash diagnostics minimize personal data.\nThese are guardrails, not approval of a stack, vendor, backend, account model,\ndata-retention policy, or technical promise.\n\n## Open decisions requiring human approval\n\n1. **Offline versus connected scope:** Reconcile the offline single-player\n   premise with required account identity, multiplayer safety, live operations,\n   and purchase/entitlement handling. Classify each as release-critical,\n   optional connected enhancement, or deferred.\n2. **Account and data policy:** Decide whether accounts are absent, optional,\n   or mandatory; then define authentication, sync/conflict behavior, retention,\n   deletion, and offline/service-outage recovery.\n3. **Multiplayer, commerce, and safety:** State whether multiplayer or\n   real-money purchases actually ship, plus age rating, regions, moderation,\n   reporting, refunds, store, and applicable legal obligations.\n4. **Technical delivery baseline:** Choose engine/framework, repository and CI\n   constraints, browser/device matrix, performance budget, offline-storage\n   quota, update activation policy, error-reporting provider, and cost model.\n5. **Production content and access:** Approve party size, encounter/quest\n   counts, combat/progression balance, language set, audio scope, and the exact\n   accessibility options before production planning.\n# Canonical specification index\n\nThis directory is the canonical, resumable specification for **mock-fantasy-quest-4**. It consolidates F001\u2013F010 without overriding [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), which remains the source of truth. A later author must cite stable IDs here rather than rediscovering scope.\n\n## Control rules\n\n- **P0** is an original, offline-capable 2D single-player fantasy RPG: one town, one dungeon, a short guided campaign and ending, exploration, dialogue/quests, party turn combat, inventory, local saves/recovery, settings/accessibility, responsive web, and PWA distribution.\n- Target-neutral rules live in `requirements.*`; the web/PWA differences live in `platform-appendix.md`. PWA is a delivery adaptation of the shared game, not a second game.\n- `Final Fantasy V` is a functional scope reference for party-based turn adventure only. It permits no copied expression: names, lore, text, art, music, maps, layouts, UI flows, or interaction choreography.\n- `proposed` requirements and every `GATE-*` are not implementation authorisation. Resolve their linked `DEC-*` record before allocating dependent work.\n\n## Chapter register\n\n| Chapter ID | Document / owner | Depends on | State |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SPEC-00 | `00-domain-discovery.md` / product discovery | INITIAL | complete |\n| SPEC-01 | `01-product-brief.md` / product | SPEC-00 | complete |\n| SPEC-02 | `02-feature-model.md` / game design | SPEC-00\u201301 | complete |\n| SPEC-03 | `03-technical-contract.md` / technical design | SPEC-00\u201302 | complete |\n| SPEC-04 | `04-experience-contract.md` / experience design | SPEC-00\u201303 | complete |\n| SPEC-11 | `requirements.md`, `requirements.json` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-00\u201304, planning 05\u201310 | complete |\n| SPEC-12 | `traceability-matrix.md` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-11 | complete; runbook IDs pending F012+ |\n| SPEC-13 | `decision-log.md` / product owner | SPEC-00\u201304 | open decisions recorded |\n| SPEC-14 | `platform-appendix.md` / technical + experience owner | SPEC-11 | complete; thresholds gated |\n| SPEC-15 | `domain-completeness-review.md` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-00\u201304, SPEC-11 | complete; gated domains retained |\n\n## Bounded expansion protocol\n\nExpand one domain chapter at a time, preserving IDs and this register: identify entities/rules, authoring inputs, dependencies, P0 boundary, target adaptations, verification, and open decisions. Proposed detailed chapters are shell/session, campaign content, combat, progression/inventory, persistence/recovery, experience/accessibility, PWA/release, and connected-scope holds. A chapter can move from `planned` to `complete` only when its requirements, trace rows, and decision/risk links exist; it never closes a human gate.\nsed: projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/README.md: No such file or directory\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification | rg '(TEMPLATE\\\\.md|F014\\\\.md|03-technical-contract\\\\.md|platform-appendix\\\\.md|R0002\\\\.md)' && sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/TEMPLATE.md && sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/F014.md && sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0002.md && rg -n -C 3 'IMP-0001|GATE-0001|baseline|repository|command|ownership' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n exited 1 in 0ms:\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0002.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md\nsed: projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/TEMPLATE.md: No such file or directory\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files . | rg '(\"'^|/)(TEMPLATE|F014)'\"\\\\.md\"'$|runbooks/R0002'\"\\\\.md\"'$'\"' && sed -n '1,240p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0002.md && rg -n -C 3 'IMP-0001|GATE-0001|baseline|repository|command|ownership' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n./runbooks/F014.md\n./runbooks/TEMPLATE.md\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0002.md\n---\ntask_id: R0002\nsequence: 2\ntitle: Enforce shared-core and adapter dependency seams\ndependencies:\n  - R0001\nrequirement_ids:\n  - NFR-0010\n  - REQ-0013\n  - NFR-0012\nsource_specifications:\n  - specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\n  - specification/03-technical-contract.md#dependency-direction-and-state-rules\nsource_catalogue_ids:\n  - IMP-0002\nauthoring_batch: B0002\nfactory_stages:\n  - F011\n  - F012\n  - F013\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids:\nasset_brief: No assets; this is an interface and dependency-policy contract.\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids:\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nCreate the dependency policy and public contracts that keep the deterministic\nshared core independent of browser, renderer, clock, network, and platform\nmodules. Adapters may submit commands and render returned state/events only.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nR0001 is an implementation dependency. Author and validate this contract now;\ndo not treat GATE-0001 as permission to implement or as a blocker to authoring.\n\n## Ownership\n\n- `src/core/public interfaces` and the `Command`, state, event, and adapter interfaces.\n- `src/platform/{web,pwa}/adapter interfaces` and the dependency-policy manifest.\n- `architecture dependency rules` and `docs/architecture.md`.\n\n## Behaviour\n\nDefine an enforceable import direction in which the core accepts shared\ncommands, validates and returns state/events, while web and PWA adapters\ntranslate platform input and render those returned values. The adapter must not\nmutate campaign state directly. Keep the policy framework-neutral so a later\ntool decision can choose its enforcement mechanism without changing the seam.\n\n## Web and PWA differences\n\nBoth targets consume the same core contract. The web adapter owns browser\ninput, rendering, and viewport integration; the PWA adapter additionally owns\ninstallation and cache-lifecycle adaptation. Neither target imports platform\nconcerns into the core or becomes a campaign authority.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nDo not implement combat, exploration, screens, campaign rules, accounts,\nnetwork clients, renderer behaviour, or target-specific rules. Do not create\nassets, artwork, fonts, icons, audio, maps, or copied reference styling.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\n`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`. This interface and dependency-policy work\nmust not create incidental product assets; `asset_ids` remains blank.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Add a dependency-rule test that rejects forbidden core imports.\n- Add a fixture adapter proving command-only interaction and returned\n  state/event rendering.\n- Record the selected enforcement mechanism only after the retained GATE-0001\n  tool decision; do not claim that gate is resolved here.\n\n## Evidence\n\nPreserve the dependency-rule test result, the fixture-adapter result, and an\narchitecture document showing the one-way core/adapter boundary.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Forbidden imports into the core are rejected.\n- A fixture adapter can submit commands and consume returned state/events\n  without directly mutating campaign state.\n- The shared contract remains usable by web and PWA adapters without choosing\n  implementation tooling or creating product assets.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-16-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-17-| Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-18-| --- | --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:19:| Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-20-| Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-21-| Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-22-| Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:23:| Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-24-| PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-25-| Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-26-\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-29-```text\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-30-authored content \u2500\u2500> content validator \u2500\u2500> deterministic rules core <\u2500\u2500 input actions\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-31-                                            \u2502\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:32:web/PWA shell + renderer <\u2500\u2500 state/events \u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500> save/recovery repository\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-33-        \u2502                                   \u2502\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-34-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500> PWA release manager \u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500> error boundary / local diagnostics\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-35-```\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-36-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-37-Only the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:38:ephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-39-owns only serialized snapshots and recovery metadata. Rendering, input,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-40-caching, and diagnostics must not be required to make a legal game decision.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-41-\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-71-```\nprojects/mock-fantasy-q